1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,920
 All right, in the last of our series on non-mechanical properties, we're going to talk about

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:09,120
 biocompatibility, the suitability of materials for use in contact with living tissue.

3
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:13,200
 While biocompatibility is particularly important for those of you who are medical engineers,

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,840
 it's not only important to medical engineers, because almost all of us will at some point

5
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:22,560
 design a component that's going to come into contact with a human. And even if you're lucky enough

6
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:26,320
 never to have to design a component to come into contact with a human, I say that because

7
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:30,080
 humans are generally not very good things to have floating around in the engineering problem,

8
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:34,560
 they're quite soft and squishy and not well defined. Even if you don't have to design something

9
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:37,760
 that's going to come into contact with a human, it may be the case that you have to package

10
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:42,800
 your transport or send or install something that may then come into contact with a human.

11
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:47,680
 So it's relevant really to all of us. There are many, many elements to biocompatibility,

12
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:52,080
 and I am by no means an expert in this. My studies were in mechanical engineering,

13
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:56,480
 and if I'm being honest, I find the human body a little bit gross and this whole topic makes

14
00:00:56,480 --> 00:01:01,280
 me feel a little bit uncomfortable. And so this video is really going to be a very, very brief

15
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:05,600
 introduction. I'm going to try and make sure that I link you to plenty of relevant resources on the

16
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:09,360
 textbook page for this video. And I'm also going to try and make sure that we hit on this topic

17
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:14,480
 in a case study this semester. The biocompatibility properties of a material are largely concerned

18
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:20,320
 with their toxicity. In other words, whether these materials pose a risk or harm to human beings

19
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:24,960
 or other living organisms. And one that you're probably familiar with is carcinogenicity.

20
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:29,680
 If a material is a carcinogen, it means that material has a tendency or a likelihood to

21
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:34,640
 promote the formation of cancers. My understanding is that carcinogens are normally classified

22
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:40,720
 as genotoxic or non-genotoxic, the first kind of affecting DNA and the latter not so much.

23
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:45,360
 But you should be aware that many engineering materials and manufacturing processes involve

24
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:49,280
 carcinogens in one way or another. Now don't be too terrified by that,

25
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:53,840
 quit engineering and enroll in an art degree. In many cases, the underlying risk is only present

26
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:59,040
 if a material is in a particular form or while that material is being transported or installed.

27
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:03,920
 There are many materials that fall into this category and are carcinogenic in particular circumstances

28
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:08,960
 but are quite inert otherwise. Perhaps the most famous of these or certainly one that's been part

29
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:14,000
 of our vernacular lately is asbestos. Asbestos is a fibrous material that was used in the building

30
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,200
 industry for many years. And actually as an engineering material, asbestos has some pretty nice

31
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:24,400
 properties. It's a great thermal and electrical insulator and it was reasonably cheap and abundant.

32
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:28,800
 Not too long ago though, it was discovered that asbestos is a carcinogen and it was outlawed for

33
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:33,680
 use in new buildings in much of the developed world. That being said, many buildings that contain

34
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:38,880
 asbestos are still deemed as perfectly safe to work in because as long as it's not disturbed,

35
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:43,760
 it doesn't pose a huge risk to humans. Now asbestos certainly does pose a risk when it's not

36
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:49,520
 left undisturbed and as a result, removal of asbestos is quite an expensive and labor-intensive

37
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:54,400
 and time-intensive process to make sure that humans are adequately shielded from it as it's

38
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:59,600
 carefully extracted from buildings and disposed of safely. There are some engineering materials

39
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:03,760
 that are carcinogenic all of the time and you need to be a little bit careful about these.

40
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:09,040
 As always, good practices to check the material safety data sheet MSDS for any material

41
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:13,040
 you're interested in using and to use these materials in the ways that are intended.

42
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:18,320
 Our next bio-compatibility property is immunogenicity. This is the tendency of a foreign body or

43
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:22,720
 substance to provoke and immune reaction. Immunogenicity is quite important to consider if you're

44
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:26,880
 selecting materials for an implant. If, for example, you're designing a synthetic joint and you

45
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:31,200
 get this wrong and you put a material in somebody's body that triggers an immune response,

46
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:35,200
 then a process known as rejection can occur where the human body will attack the implant as

47
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:40,160
 if it were an infection. Many bad things can happen if this occurs, the least of which are

48
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:45,200
 inflammation and loosening or damage to the implants. In addition to its excellent mechanical

49
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:49,920
 properties, this is one of the reasons why titanium is quite a common choice in dental and other

50
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:54,480
 medical implants, for example, it doesn't tend to provoke an immune response. The final

51
00:03:54,480 --> 00:04:00,080
 analysis of bio-compatibility material properties is teratogenicity. This is the tendency of a material

52
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:05,760
 or a substance to cause birth defects on an embryo or a fetus. Ionizing radiation, as well as

53
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:10,800
 some materials, can be teratogenic. And you should be aware that byproducts of some engineering

54
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:16,000
 processes, manufacture and installation can also be. This leads us to a bigger point around risk

55
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,680
 management. Our modern engineering world is ripe with biological hazards that are out to get us.

56
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:27,360
 Castanogenic toxic and teratogenic substances are byproducts of many of the engineering manufacturing

57
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:32,080
 processes that we rely on. In general, the engineering risk management framework and hierarchy

58
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:36,880
 of controls are important in helping you minimize exposure to humans. If possible, you first

59
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:42,160
 remove the hazard. If not, then you substitute it. If not, then you implement some sort of engineering

60
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:47,120
 or administrative control. And finally, if exposure to these materials is absolutely necessary,

61
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:51,040
 you equip the person with the appropriate protective equipment to make sure that they're going to be

62
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:55,440
 okay. In general, when dealing with a material that has the potential to cause harm to another human

63
00:04:55,920 --> 00:05:01,120
 your obligation, your responsibility is to try and provide a dose which is as low as reasonably

64
00:05:01,120 --> 00:05:06,240
 achievable. And if you're using a non-standard material or manufacturing process, you have an

65
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:11,120
 obligation as an engineer to raise awareness and make sure that all of the risks are covered.

66
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:15,360
 Some of the mechanical and non-mechanical properties that we've already met are also important

67
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:20,160
 in this context. For example, material selection of synthetic joints depends not only on those

68
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:24,800
 things being biocompatible, but also on those things being mechanically sufficient.

69
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:28,880
 And interestingly, considerations must be made not just to the outright performance of these

70
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:33,440
 materials in terms of their properties, but also to matching these properties to the surrounding

71
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:38,240
 human tissue. A mismatch of stiffness, for example, between the implant and the tissue you can lead

72
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:43,600
 to premature cracking of one of these things. Obviously, corrosion resistance is also important

73
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:49,040
 if you're putting something inside of the human body, which is often warm and wet and corrosive.

74
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:52,720
 This is one of the reasons why materials that have a tendency for

75
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:57,440
 passivity, stainless steels, aluminium, titanium, are quite often used as well.

76
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:01,440
 We'll talk a little bit more about this in a medical-specific case study a little bit later in the

77
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:03,440
 semester.
